  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 300 PM CST Tue Nov 3 2026\n\nHigh confidence in less than a half inch of rain on Thursday:\n\nThe upper-level low currently entering the west coast is\nforecast to eject into the central Plains on Thursday. Lift and\na diffuse cold front associated with the wave will bring 20-50%\nchances of rain to the area. Any rain appears to be light in\nnature as any appreciable moisture is forecast to be locked in\nthe Gulf by the cold front moving through today. Every LREF\ncluster keeps mean totals below a quarter inch. 90% of models\ndepict widespread totals less than half an inch.\n\n\nDry and seasonable weather through the weekend:\n\nAfter Thursday's wave, yet another upper-level trough is\nforecast to move through during the weekend. With the resulting\njet stream just south of the area, and the trough axis only\ngiving a glancing blow to our region, temperatures are expected\nto stay seasonable. Highs are forecast to be in the middle 50s\nto middle 60s Friday through Sunday, with lows in the lower 30s\nto lower 40s. The lower 30s are expected to be in the eastern\nOzarks, largely keeping areas west of Highway 63 above freezing.\n\n\nA warm-up to above normal temperatures looks likely next week:\n\nAfter the series of waves, ensembles are in fairly good\nagreement on ridging to take hold of the central and west CONUS\ngoing into next week. This will bring another warm up to above\nnormal temperatures with highs reaching the 70s by Tuesday. NBM\ninterquartile spreads keep temperatures in the 70s through at\nleast the 12th, and the CPC agrees with their forecast for\nNovember 11-16 showing a 60-70% chance for above normal\ntemperatures.\n\nSince forecast lows are currently supposed to stay above \nfreezing for the Springfield area and many other areas across \nthe CWA, this technically means the growing season is forecast\nto last into mid-November. For some context, September was our\nwarmest on record. October was our second warmest on record. And\nabove normal temperatures in November could keep these top\nwarmest months going. Furthermore, MRCC data shows that Greene\nCounty's latest first freeze date on record is November 12th. \nIf the warm temperatures indeed pan out, we could set a new \nlatest first freeze date. Even furthermore, Greene County's\nearliest last freeze date is March 12th. This year, our last\nfreeze was March 18th. Therefore, if the warm temperatures pan\nout, we could be looking at a record long \"growing season\" this\nyear. Though it has been a bit too dry to grow much of anything\nlately.",
